WHY THIS, FOR NOW

An open licence is a one-way door. Permissions can always be added later;
they cannot be taken back once granted, because anyone who received the work
under the old terms keeps them forever. Since this game is pre-alpha and its
commercial future is undecided, the reversible choice is the correct one.

Nothing is lost by waiting. Nobody forks an unfinished game, so an open
licence today would buy no contributors while closing off options.

This file exists so the position is explicit. A repository with no LICENSE
file at all reads as an oversight and leaves a reader genuinely unsure what
they may do. "Not yet decided" is a decision, and this is it written down.

WHAT YOU MAY DO WITHOUT ASKING

Read it. Play it at your own table. Talk about it, quote from it in a review
or discussion, and tell people what you think.

Those need no licence from anyone. Game mechanics and systems are not
protected by copyright; only specific expression is. Nothing here stops you
running these rules with your own group.

WHEN THIS GETS REVISITED

When the game is playable end to end, which means the Age I, III and IV
action tables ported and at least one full playthrough of the published
rules rather than the family edition.

At that point the choice is between two clear paths:

  - To invite community hacks and derivative work: CC BY-SA 4.0, or the ORC
    licence, which was built for tabletop games specifically and is the more
    natural fit for this hobby.

  - To sell a finished edition: keep rights reserved and release
    commercially. Being the sole copyright holder means this stays available
    even after choosing openness for the text, via dual licensing.

Deciding now would be guessing at an answer that costs nothing to defer.

ON THE ORIGINAL

This game is a declared hack of Worldwizard by Jason Lutes, published by
Lampblack & Brimstone, which is itself built on Dawn of Worlds by N. Bob
Pesall. All text in this repository is original. See CREDITS.md, and buy the
original for $5 at https://lampblack-brimstone.itch.io/worldwizard

Whatever licence is eventually chosen here applies only to this repository's
own text. It cannot and does not grant anyone rights to Worldwizard.
